.TH @APP@ 1 "@DATE@" "odox @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
@APP@ \- @SUMMARY@
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B @APP@
.RI [ FILE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
@DESCRIPTION@
.PP
The document is read from the file and nothing is sent anywhere. Editing means
changing what is there, not authoring: text can be typed and deleted, a
paragraph split or joined, a cell given a value, a shape moved or resized, any
of it undone, and the document saved. Formatting, inserting, formulas and find
and replace are an office suite's. What was not touched is written back as it
was read, element for element, so the saved document is the opened one with the
change in it. Nothing is written until Save, and then only the file that was
opened or the one named; before it is, what is about to be written is read back
and compared with the document in the window, and a difference refuses the save.
.SH OPTIONS
There are none. A path given on the command line is opened at startup; a
document may also be dropped on the window or chosen with Ctrl+O.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Ctrl+O
Open a document.
.TP
.B Ctrl+R
Read the open document from disk again.
.TP
.B Ctrl+W
Close it.
.TP
.B Ctrl+S, Ctrl+Shift+S
Save over the open file; save somewhere else.
.TP
.B Ctrl+Z, Ctrl+Shift+Z
Take an edit back; put it back.
.TP
.B Ctrl+E
Edit mode on or off. A window opens reading unless the preference in the Edit
menu says otherwise.
.TP
.B Ctrl+plus, Ctrl+minus, Ctrl+0
Zoom in, zoom out, and back to actual size.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/odox/settings.toml
The one preference, whether a document opens in edit mode, written only when it
is changed in the menu.
.I ~/.config
where
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset. @APP@ writes no cache and no temporary copy; a save goes through a
.I .part
file beside the target that is renamed over it.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR xodt (1),
.BR xods (1),
.BR xodp (1)
.PP
Each reads one kind of OpenDocument file and names its sibling when handed
another.
.SH AUTHOR
David M. Anderson.
